Mechanism
launcher.rs:14 — explicit right/down capture the GUI owner's selected tab, focused anchor and process generation once; absent or cross-swarm anchors refuse before launch. Tab and fresh swarm space use existing placement types. Requested name, role and cwd survive in the full SpawnSpec.
proto/gui.rs:120 — UiSpawned binds the child operation/seat/swarm and captured placement to captured and applied layout revisions. Revisions are provenance; the existing model's same-tab and anchor-generation guards decide placement.
cockpit.rs:141 — SpawnSpec participates in the ordinary shared spawn admission cap, alongside declarative and legacy launches. Static review found and corrected its initial omission; the cap regression now mixes all three forms. This follow-up has not run yet.
main.rs:3765 — freeze the observed Placed seat before ConsumeSpawn can remove the retained model outcome. The produced-frame reply requires that exact seat to be focused and present in the encoded layout.
main.rs:3812 — ordinary layout cache has no stale per-operation receipt; spawn receipt is added only to the operation reply after its frame. Accepted close Target.ready/shown checks remain in the shared waiter.
main.rs:3981 — begin_ui supplies the optional captured spawn to the shared frame waiter; ordinary close passes None. No additional reader, socket or rendering path.
launcher.rs:212 — quiet-window test strengthening carries a captured request through later focus and model publication, checking the original anchor and pre-existing 0.37 sibling ratio. Written after the earlier 3/0 run; not executed yet.
tests/headless.rs:109 — real six-seat private file-account regression covers right/down/tab/space, sibling rectangles, captured/applied revision, idempotent retry after focus changes, cwd and an image after the placement frame.
scripts/e2e/L2.sh:1 — consumes the runner's existing private no-window app; owns no lifecycle. Header and canonical map agree: partial; missing 'never a guessed slot'. The pending-spawn focus race has no night witness yet. No task number invented.

